  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $F(x)=(2x+1){e}^{2x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-2)?$\n\n$f(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(2(-x)+1){e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & =(-2x+1){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2(-2)+1){e}^{-2(-2)}=5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
